Astrophysical simulations traditionally separate fluid dynamics, magnetic field evolution, topology, and data integrity into distinct computational domains. This white sheet introduces a unified architecture — the Curvature‑Flux Helix Engine — that couples geometric curvature, torsion, and flux density directly to magnetorotational instability (MRI), shear‑driven stress transformation, and exotic‑matter stability encoding. The system integrates a cryptographically self‑verifying simulation pipeline using sheared‑key cryptosystems to ensure provenance and reproducibility. Conceptual testing indicates the emergence of geometry‑dependent MRI zones, stable exotic‑matter candidate pockets, and topological invariants not present in baseline MHD simulations. This architecture proposes a new class of astrophysical simulation capable of predicting field‑shaped matter and generating secure scientific artifacts.

2. Introduction

Astrophysical environments such as accretion disks exhibit complex interactions between magnetic fields, turbulence, shear, and curvature. Standard magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) solvers treat these interactions as linear or weakly coupled phenomena. However, observational and theoretical evidence suggests that curvature, torsion, and flux geometry play a significant role in shaping MRI behavior, shock formation, and matter stability.

Simultaneously, scientific reproducibility and data integrity remain major challenges in high‑performance simulation environments. Existing pipelines lack cryptographic provenance, leaving results vulnerable to tampering or ambiguity in lineage.

This white sheet presents a unified architecture that addresses both challenges: a simulation engine that couples geometry, flux, and shear to MRI physics, and a cryptographic layer that produces self‑verifying scientific artifacts.

